    {
      "id": "sn1987a",
      "name": "SN 1987A",
      "summary": "The closest supernova seen since the telescope was invented, in the Large Magellanic Cloud in 1987. Its star was a compact blue supergiant, not the usual red one, so it brightened slowly to a dome peaking about three months later instead of holding a plateau. The lab classes it II-P; astronomers call it a peculiar Type II (II-pec). The lab reaches a blue supergiant only at low metal content, so this preset uses 0.1 of the Sun's (the LMC itself has about half); the real star was more likely made blue by a merger with a companion.",
      "whatWasSeen": "About three hours before the first light, Kamiokande-II caught 11 neutrinos in 13 seconds, IMB 8 and Baksan 5: 24 in all, still the only neutrinos ever seen from a supernova. The light peaked near magnitude 3 after about 85 days, and gamma-ray lines from radioactive cobalt and, decades later, titanium showed the radioactive heating directly. From about 1995 the blast began hitting a ring of gas the star had shed thousands of years earlier, lighting it up in X-rays. In 2024 JWST found signs of a hidden neutron star.",
      "inputs": { "kind": "MassiveStar", "initialMass": 19, "metallicity": 0.1, "opacity": 0.10 },
      "observer": {
        "distancePc": 51400, "av": 0.47,
        "source": { "citation": "Distance 51.4 ± 1.2 kpc from the ring geometry (Panagia 2005, IAU Colloq. 192); A_V = 3.1 × E(B−V) 0.15 (Blinnikov et al. 2000, ApJ 532, 1132, after Wampler 1988) [DERIVED]", "researchRef": "presets §1; presets-verification" }
      },
      "pins": {
